{"page":646,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.33117,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"632 Chapter 10: Infinite Sequences and Series"},{"x":0.35277,"y":0.09302,"w":0.44322,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"When we state Taylor’s theorem this way, it says that for each x∊I,"},{"x":0.54589,"y":0.11604,"w":0.14379,"h":0.01498,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(x) = Pn(x) + Rn(x)."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.13786,"w":0.59302,"h":0.06071,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"The function Rn(x) is determined by the value of the (n + 1)st derivative ƒ(n+1) at a point c that depends on both a and x, and that lies somewhere between them. For any value of n we want, the equation gives both a polynomial approximation of ƒ of that order and a for- mula for the error involved in using that approximation over the interval I."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.20111,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02967,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Equation (1) is called Taylor’s formula. The function Rn(x) is called the remainder of order n or the error term for the approximation of ƒ by Pn(x) over I."},{"x":0.35358,"y":0.25796,"w":0.52841,"h":0.0301,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"If Rn(x) S 0 as n S q for all x∊I, we say that the Taylor series generated by ƒ at x = a converges to ƒ on I, and we write"},{"x":0.6037,"y":0.2976,"w":0.03947,"h":0.01422,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(k)(a)"},{"x":0.6171,"y":0.30709,"w":0.08988,"h":0.02182,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"k! (x - a)k."},{"x":0.5286,"y":0.30784,"w":0.07045,"h":0.01695,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(x) = a"},{"x":0.58037,"y":0.32262,"w":0.02002,"h":0.00916,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"k=0"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.35875,"w":0.58834,"h":0.01432,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Often we can estimate Rn without knowing the value of c, as the following example illustrates."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.3967,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02984,"fs":0.01407,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 1 Show that the Taylor series generated by ƒ(x) = ex at x = 0 converges to ƒ(x) for every real value of x."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.43865,"w":0.58814,"h":0.0301,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Solution The function has derivatives of all orders throughout the interval I = (-q, q). Equations (1) and (2) with ƒ(x) = ex and a = 0 give"},{"x":0.52258,"y":0.47674,"w":0.10039,"h":0.02903,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"2! + g + xn"},{"x":0.42198,"y":0.47676,"w":0.11401,"h":0.02141,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ex = 1 + x + x2"},{"x":0.7143,"y":0.47705,"w":0.12832,"h":0.02302,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Polynomial from Section 10.8, Example 2"},{"x":0.60956,"y":0.48469,"w":0.07119,"h":0.02107,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"n! + Rn(x)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.5199,"w":0.02362,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"and"},{"x":0.45225,"y":0.54069,"w":0.09214,"h":0.02293,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Rn(x) = ec"},{"x":0.50983,"y":0.54743,"w":0.30408,"h":0.02229,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"(n + 1)!xn+1 for some c between 0 and x."},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.57485,"w":0.5931,"h":0.05041,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Since ex is an increasing function of x, ec lies between e0 = 1 and ex. When x is negative, so is c, and ec 6 1. When x is zero, ex = 1 so that Rn(x) = 0. When x is positive, so is c, and ec 6 ex. Thus, for Rn(x) given as above,"},{"x":0.46378,"y":0.62817,"w":0.12415,"h":0.02625,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"\u0002 Rn(x) \u0002 … \u0002 x\u0002 n+1"},{"x":0.53732,"y":0.63496,"w":0.26639,"h":0.02556,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"(n + 1)! when x … 0, ec 6 1"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.6657,"w":0.02362,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"and"},{"x":0.46089,"y":0.68603,"w":0.13035,"h":0.02338,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"\u0002 Rn(x) \u0002 6 ex xn+1"},{"x":0.54858,"y":0.68996,"w":0.25802,"h":0.02556,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"(n + 1)! when x 7 0. ec 6 ex"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.72581,"w":0.10525,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Finally, because"},{"x":0.50197,"y":0.74614,"w":0.02828,"h":0.01466,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xn+1"},{"x":0.4876,"y":0.75455,"w":0.39641,"h":0.02108,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"(n + 1)! = 0 for every x, Section 10.1, Theorem 5"},{"x":0.45646,"y":0.75522,"w":0.02896,"h":0.02012,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"lim nSq"},{"x":0.32414,"y":0.78448,"w":0.41547,"h":0.02155,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"lim nSqRn(x) = 0, and the series converges to ex for every x. Thus,"},{"x":0.52086,"y":0.81565,"w":0.01257,"h":0.0142,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xk"},{"x":0.62209,"y":0.81565,"w":0.09961,"h":0.02903,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"2! + g + xk"},{"x":0.5208,"y":0.81567,"w":0.1147,"h":0.02901,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"k! = 1 + x + x2"},{"x":0.45992,"y":0.81912,"w":0.05623,"h":0.02142,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ex = a"},{"x":0.70908,"y":0.82361,"w":0.2032,"h":0.02106,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"k! + g. (3)"},{"x":0.49748,"y":0.83839,"w":0.02002,"h":0.00916,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"k=0"},{"x":0.05789,"y":0.87358,"w":0.15989,"h":0.01163,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The Number e as a Series"},{"x":0.35277,"y":0.88499,"w":0.36443,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"We can use the result of Example 1 with x = 1 to write"},{"x":0.15799,"y":0.89783,"w":0.01223,"h":0.0248,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1 n!"},{"x":0.1058,"y":0.90381,"w":0.04626,"h":0.01525,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"e = a"},{"x":0.50255,"y":0.90805,"w":0.10761,"h":0.01982,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"e = 1 + 1 + 1"},{"x":0.59926,"y":0.90805,"w":0.16322,"h":0.02744,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"2! + g + 1 n! + Rn(1),"},{"x":0.13495,"y":0.91711,"w":0.01854,"h":0.00824,"fs":0.00783,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"n=0"}]}